+## Release anchor (unattended releases without an owner signature each)
+
+This mode is **optional**. Without any new contract, the owner can already move the name by setting `value` to a
+fixed `/ipfs/<CID>` with `enclave config set`: one owner signature per release, on the existing ledger. The release
+anchor is a proposed, independent additive contract for the case where CI should release unattended, with a key that
+can choose only the CID. Its design document compares the options, including the existing EnclaveAddressBook.
+
+With `releaseAnchor`, the published value is whatever an on-chain
+`ReleaseAnchor` contract holds. The contract, its forge tests and the
+delegate's release tool live in the enclave repository under
+[`tools/ipns-release-anchor/`](https://github.com/EnclaveHost/enclave/blob/main/tools/ipns-release-anchor) (the contract is
+[`src/ReleaseAnchor.sol`](https://github.com/EnclaveHost/enclave/blob/main/tools/ipns-release-anchor/src/ReleaseAnchor.sol)). The owner appoints a delegate, for example a CI key, whose only
+power is `publish(cid, expectedSeq)`, for at most 180 days. The owner's
+`pin(cid)` overrides the delegate and revokes it in one transaction. The
+publisher's config, secrets and key are out of the delegate's reach. The
+protocol, its safety and liveness claims, and what it does not bind (other
+holders of the IPNS key) are in the design document,
+[`tools/ipns-release-anchor/docs/ipns-release-anchor.md`](https://github.com/EnclaveHost/enclave/blob/main/tools/ipns-release-anchor/docs/ipns-release-anchor.md)
+in the enclave repository; the production cutover tooling is
+[`tools/ipns-cutover/`](https://github.com/EnclaveHost/enclave/blob/main/tools/ipns-cutover).
+
+```json
+"releaseAnchor": {
+  "chainId": 8453,
+  "contract": "0x…",
+  "codeHash": "0x…",
+  "rpcs": ["https://a…", "https://b…", "https://$RPC_C_URL"],
+  "quorum": 2,
+  "confirmations": 6,
+  "maxBlockAgeSecs": 120,
+  "authorityTtlSecs": 43200,
+  "pollSecs": 120,
+  "sequenceOffset": 1000,
+  "rpcTimeoutSecs": 10
+}
+```
+
+### Reading (`src/anchor.rs`)
+
+Every `pollSecs` the publisher runs one quorum round, one endpoint per
+event-loop tick. It asks all endpoints, never stopping at the first answer:
+
+1. `eth_chainId` and `eth_blockNumber`.
+2. It takes the quorum-th highest head minus `confirmations`, and needs
+   `quorum` identical (number, hash, timestamp) answers for that block.
+3. By that `blockHash` (EIP-1898, `requireCanonical`) it reads the code and
+   `state()`:
+   - the runtime must hash to `codeHash`, which `scripts/expected-codehash.mjs`
+     derives from the reviewed build;
+   - `quorum` call results must be identical.
+4. Then the checks:
+   - the block must be at most `maxBlockAgeSecs` old by the clock at the
+     **end** of the round;
+   - the schema and the name binding (`nameHash` = keccak256 of this key's
+     peer multihash) must match;
+   - neither the block nor the seq may fall below the high-water, which is
+     kept in `/data` when there is one.
+
+A round proves the anchor state at one recent, canonical block. It does not
+prove "the latest" state: endpoints can withhold the newest blocks, up to
+the freshness window.
+
+Endpoints must be distinct providers, by the same rule as the enclave
+repository's `network/rpc-identity.mjs`. A public host is **one** provider
+whatever its port, path, case or trailing dot, so `https://rpc.example`
+and `https://rpc.example:8443/other` cannot make a quorum of two. IPv4-mapped
+and NAT64 IPv6 addresses count as their IPv4 host. Loopback spellings
+(`localhost`, `127.0.0.0/8`, `[::1]`) on one port are one local listener.
+
+URLs follow a strict grammar (`parse_rpc_url` in `src/anchor.rs`):
+- no credentials, query, fragment, percent-escapes, backslash, whitespace
+  or non-ASCII;
+- a numeric host must be a dotted-quad IPv4 address (`127.1` and
+  `0x7f.0.0.1` are refused);
+- a port is 1..65535.
+
+https is required, except for a *parsed* loopback address or exactly
+`localhost`. A public name like `127.evil.example` is not loopback. There is
+no default list and no fallback. Provider keys may sit in the URL path, set
+through `$ENV` references. Logs and status only show the host and port; the
+URL that is dialed is rebuilt from the parsed parts.
+
+### Signing
+
+- Value and sequence:
+  - the value is `/ipfs/<cid>`;
+  - the IPNS sequence is `sequenceOffset + seq`, with overflow refused.
+- Caps from the authority:
+  - EOL = min(now + `lifetimeSecs`, block timestamp + `authorityTtlSecs`);
+  - the record TTL is capped by both.
+- Nothing is signed when:
+  - no round has succeeded;
+  - the authority is about to expire;
+  - the anchor is retired;
+  - a recovered record (from a delegate, the DHT or the state file) has a
+    higher sequence, or the same sequence with another value.
+
+So a superseded release, including a revoked delegate's, stops validating at
+most `authorityTtlSecs` after the block that superseded it. This holds even
+if the publisher is partitioned from every RPC, or its clock is wrong. While
+partitioned, it re-signs only within that bound, then **stops signing**.
+That is the liveness cost.
+
+`POST /publish` in this mode starts a fresh round; it never signs from cached
+state on demand. `/api/status` reports:
+- `authorityMode`;
+- `configError`;
+- `anchor`: the authority, the per-endpoint outcomes, the high-water,
+  `lastError`, and the round counters.
+
+### Cutover from a fixed value
+
+Choose `sequenceOffset` so that `sequenceOffset + 1` is above every
+sequence the name ever had. The publisher refuses a lower mapping it can
+see, but it cannot see records it cannot reach. To go back, remove
+`releaseAnchor` and set `value`: the legacy behaviour is unchanged.
+
+`ipns-publisher id <key>` prints the `anchor-name-hash` the contract's
+constructor needs.
 
